[
    {
        "content": "<p>I had a look at the definition of <code>Equiv</code> (from Mathlib.Logic.Equiv.Defs)</p>\n<p>I understand (or I thought I understood) the description that is given in the documentation: an equivalence between two types <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> is made from two functions, one from <code>a</code> to <code>b</code> and the other from <code>b</code> to <code>a</code>, together with two proofs that these two functions are inverse of each other. These two proofs are <code>left_inv</code> and <code>right_inv</code>. I even know where I got this notion from: that's exactly how it's described in Chapter 7 of Mathematics in Lean.</p>\n<p>But now when I try to open the file in VScode, I see that (it seems to me) the actual definition gives a <em>proof</em>  of both <code>left_inv</code> and <code>right_inv</code>. This can't be right, because this cannot be proved of arbitrary functions - so I'm understanding something wrong.</p>\n<p>So most likely, I am misunderstanding what I'm reading. This, at least, proves I'm still where I belong in \"new members\".</p>\n<p>(To be fair, the code also uses some keyword <code>protected</code> that I'm unfamiliar with, and it's quite possible that this unfamiliarity is the ultimate source of my misunderstanding)</p>\n<p>So, how should one construct such an equivalence, and what am I getting wrong?</p>",
        "id": 624929261,
        "sender_full_name": "Philippe Duchon",
        "timestamp": 1789645655
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The proof there is the \"default proof\". It doesn't always work. It is provided for convenience in case it works, so downstream doesn't need to write it</p>",
        "id": 624929779,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1789645812
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Your understanding in the first paragraph is totally correct</p>",
        "id": 624929828,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1789645831
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks! I am now promoting myself into \"not totally new members\".</p>",
        "id": 624930105,
        "sender_full_name": "Philippe Duchon",
        "timestamp": 1789645922
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"726077\">Philippe Duchon</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Confused.20again.20.28definition.20of.20Equiv.29/near/624929261\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the code also uses some keyword <code>protected</code> that I'm unfamiliar with</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>protected</code> means that you always have to specify the namespace when referring to the declaration, even if the namespace is <code>open</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bar1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">protected</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bar2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- info: Foo.bar1 : Nat -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bar1</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- error: Unknown identifier `bar2` -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bar2</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and for <code>Equiv</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Logic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Equiv</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Equiv</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `Perm` is not `protected`</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Perm</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- but `left_inv` is</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">left_inv</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Equiv</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">left_inv</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 624932904,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1789646658
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"726077\">Philippe Duchon</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Confused.20again.20.28definition.20of.20Equiv.29/near/624929261\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>But now when I try to open the file in VScode, I see that (it seems to me) the actual definition gives a <em>proof</em> of both <code>left_inv</code> and <code>right_inv</code>. This can't be right, because this cannot be proved of arbitrary functions - so I'm understanding something wrong.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>These default proofs are called <code>autoParam</code>s and they're a feature of core Lean. Here's a basic example of them in action:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- no autoparam</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">37</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">37</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- error: \"Fields missing: `hn`\"</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">37</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- autoparam example</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">37</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- default proof</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">37</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- another proof still works</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- no error here! Default proof works</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">37</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- error : \"could not synthesize default value for field 'hn'\"</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">38</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 625162714,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1789716379
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks! So if I understand correctly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>if I don't give a \"default proof\" in the structure definition, I <em>must</em> provide the proof when defining an instance of the structure;</li>\n<li>if I give a default proof in the structure definition, I can omit the proof when defining an instance, but then if the default proof doesn't work I'll get an error; or, I can provide the proof, and then it will be used instead of the default proof. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>And, possibly, such <code>autoParam</code>s don't have to live in <code>Prop</code>?</p>",
        "id": 625268846,
        "sender_full_name": "Philippe Duchon",
        "timestamp": 1789744047
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>All the other stuff: yes. Autoparams don't have to live in Prop -- I don't know for sure (you're probably right) and I'm not at a computer to check but note that instead of asking the question you can simply do the experiment and answer your own question.</p>",
        "id": 625314112,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1789756371
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>an example of a type-valued autoparam is (if i’ve understood correctly) in <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=tsum#doc\">docs#tsum</a></p>",
        "id": 625317499,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Waring",
        "timestamp": 1789757406
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Technically <code>autoParam</code>s are the defaults that start with <code>by</code>, otherwise they're <code>optParam</code>s.<br>\nYou can <code>#print Equiv.mk</code> and <code>#print tsum</code> to see that. Here's the difference:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">usingOptParam</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">42</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">usingAutoParam</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">42</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">OfNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ofNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">zero</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- info: 42 -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">usingOptParam</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- info: 0 -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">usingAutoParam</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>autoParam</code>s (<code>by</code> defaults) are evaluated when the function is called, but <code>optParam</code>s are evaluated when they are defined. That's why <code>by exact 42</code> uses the new <code>OfNat</code> instance that makes <code>42</code> turn into zero.</p>",
        "id": 625320079,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1789758207
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>ah! thanks very much i hadn’t picked up that difference</p>",
        "id": 625327920,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Waring",
        "timestamp": 1789760499
    }
